WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.233
A Claim Is Not Evidence: How to Review an AI

2
00:00:03.233 --> 00:00:06.467
Workflow Before You Believe It. A new AI workflow can

3
00:00:06.467 --> 00:00:09.700
look convincing in under a minute. A screen changes. A

4
00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:12.934
report appears. A creator says it saved hours, replaced a

5
00:00:12.934 --> 00:00:16.167
team, or turned a difficult task into a button. That

6
00:00:16.167 --> 00:00:19.400
may be a useful starting point. It is not yet

7
00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:22.634
enough to make a decision. A feature is not a

8
00:00:22.634 --> 00:00:25.867
result. A demonstration is not a controlled test. A creator’s

9
00:00:25.867 --> 00:00:29.101
experience is not a promise that the same thing will

10
00:00:29.101 --> 00:00:32.334
happen in a different organization, with different data, permissions, people,

11
00:00:32.334 --> 00:00:35.567
and pressure. Beacon uses a simple rule: state a conclusion

12
00:00:35.567 --> 00:00:38.801
no more strongly than the evidence supports. That is not

13
00:00:38.801 --> 00:00:42.034
a reason to avoid new tools. It is how you

14
00:00:42.034 --> 00:00:45.267
keep a promising tool from becoming an expensive misunderstanding. Four

15
00:00:45.267 --> 00:00:48.501
things that are often confused A product feature What it

16
00:00:48.501 --> 00:00:51.734
can tell you What a vendor says the product is

17
00:00:51.734 --> 00:00:54.968
designed to do. What it cannot tell you by itself

18
00:00:54.968 --> 00:00:58.201
Whether it works in your setting, with your data, under

19
00:00:58.201 --> 00:01:01.434
your rules. A demonstration What it can tell you That

20
00:01:01.434 --> 00:01:04.668
a particular example was made to work under shown conditions.

21
00:01:04.668 --> 00:01:07.901
What it cannot tell you by itself Whether the example

22
00:01:07.901 --> 00:01:11.135
is typical, repeatable, complete, or independently verified. A reported result

23
00:01:11.135 --> 00:01:14.368
What it can tell you What a named person or

24
00:01:14.368 --> 00:01:17.601
organization says happened for them. What it cannot tell you

25
00:01:17.601 --> 00:01:20.835
by itself Whether the result will transfer to another team,

26
00:01:20.835 --> 00:01:24.068
task, budget, or time period. An outcome promise What it

27
00:01:24.068 --> 00:01:27.302
can tell you What someone wants a buyer or viewer

28
00:01:27.302 --> 00:01:30.535
to expect. What it cannot tell you by itself Proof

29
00:01:30.535 --> 00:01:33.768
that the outcome is likely, suitable, lawful, or safe for

30
00:01:33.768 --> 00:01:37.002
you. Make a small evidence card before you act You

31
00:01:37.002 --> 00:01:40.235
do not need a research department to ask better questions.

32
00:01:40.235 --> 00:01:43.469
Before you connect a system, move work into it, or

33
00:01:43.469 --> 00:01:46.702
repeat a claim in public, create a short evidence card.

34
00:01:46.702 --> 00:01:49.935
Name the claim exactly. “This drafts meeting notes” is different

35
00:01:49.935 --> 00:01:53.169
from “This removes the need for a reviewer.” Name who

36
00:01:53.169 --> 00:01:56.402
is making it. A vendor, creator, employee, customer, regulator, researcher,

37
00:01:56.402 --> 00:01:59.635
or independent evaluator each carries a different kind of evidence.

38
00:01:59.635 --> 00:02:02.869
Find the date and conditions. Version, plan, permissions, data used,

39
00:02:02.869 --> 00:02:06.102
task, benchmark, budget, and human help can change the result.

40
00:02:06.102 --> 00:02:09.336
Write what remains unverified. Privacy, reliability, accessibility, support, cost, rights,

41
00:02:09.336 --> 00:02:12.569
export, recovery, and error handling are often missing from a

42
00:02:12.569 --> 00:02:15.802
polished demonstration. Choose the smallest safe next test. Use non-sensitive

43
00:02:15.802 --> 00:02:19.036
or fictional material. Give one person responsibility for review. Keep

44
00:02:19.036 --> 00:02:22.269
a rollback path. The National Institute of Standards and Technology

45
00:02:22.269 --> 00:02:25.503
describes its Generative AI Profile as a voluntary companion to

46
00:02:25.503 --> 00:02:28.736
the AI Risk Management Framework, intended to help organizations incorporate

47
00:02:28.736 --> 00:02:31.969
trustworthiness considerations across the AI lifecycle. It is not a

48
00:02:31.969 --> 00:02:35.203
certification that a product is trustworthy. It is a useful

49
00:02:35.203 --> 00:02:38.436
reminder that a decision includes more than the moment a

50
00:02:38.436 --> 00:02:41.670
tool appears to work. Why language matters in public When

51
00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:44.903
a company says a tool will save time, reduce errors,

52
00:02:44.903 --> 00:02:48.136
improve conversion, secure data, replace work, or produce a result,

53
00:02:48.136 --> 00:02:51.370
readers may reasonably hear more than enthusiasm. They may hear

54
00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:54.603
an objective claim. The Federal Trade Commission’s advertising-substantiation policy states

55
00:02:54.603 --> 00:02:57.837
that advertisers and agencies must have a reasonable basis for

56
00:02:57.837 --> 00:03:01.070
objective claims before they are disseminated. What counts as a

57
00:03:01.070 --> 00:03:04.303
reasonable basis depends on the type of claim, the product,

58
00:03:04.303 --> 00:03:07.537
the consequences of getting it wrong, the benefits of the

59
00:03:07.537 --> 00:03:10.770
claim, the cost of substantiation, and the evidence experts would

60
00:03:10.770 --> 00:03:14.003
consider reasonable. Beacon does not treat that policy statement as

61
00:03:14.003 --> 00:03:17.237
legal advice for every reader or every situation. We treat

62
00:03:17.237 --> 00:03:20.470
it as a practical editorial discipline: do not let a

63
00:03:20.470 --> 00:03:23.704
smooth sentence run farther than the proof behind it. A

64
00:03:23.704 --> 00:03:26.937
good test leaves you with more control A useful evaluation

65
00:03:26.937 --> 00:03:30.170
should make your next decision clearer. It should tell you

66
00:03:30.170 --> 00:03:33.404
what the tool saw, what it did, who reviewed it,

67
00:03:33.404 --> 00:03:36.637
what could be undone, and what evidence would make you

68
00:03:36.637 --> 00:03:39.871
pause. If a trial requires credentials, customer records, private calls,

69
00:03:39.871 --> 00:03:43.104
a payment method, or an irreversible connection before you can

70
00:03:43.104 --> 00:03:46.337
understand the basic conditions, pause. A smaller test may be

71
00:03:46.337 --> 00:03:49.571
more responsible than a bigger promise. That is not fear.

72
00:03:49.571 --> 00:03:52.804
It is how people keep the tool in its proper

73
00:03:52.804 --> 00:03:56.038
place: supporting visible work and accountable judgment rather than asking

74
00:03:56.038 --> 00:03:59.271
a demo to carry more authority than it has earned.

75
00:03:59.271 --> 00:04:02.504
Sources and boundaries This is educational commentary and a general

76
00:04:02.504 --> 00:04:05.738
evidence-literacy framework. It is not legal, privacy, security, financial, medical,

77
00:04:05.738 --> 00:04:08.971
or professional advice; it does not certify a tool, workflow,

78
00:04:08.971 --> 00:04:12.205
vendor, or claim. Product capabilities, pricing, terms, access, and documentation

79
00:04:12.205 --> 00:04:15.438
can change. Re-open primary records before relying on them. National

80
00:04:15.438 --> 00:04:18.671
Institute of Standards and Technology, Artificial Intelligence Risk Management Framework:

81
00:04:18.671 --> 00:04:21.905
Generative Artificial Intelligence Profile (NIST AI 600-1) (published July 26,

82
00:04:21.905 --> 00:04:25.138
2024; accessed October 7, 2026). Federal Trade Commission, FTC Policy

83
00:04:25.138 --> 00:04:28.372
Statement Regarding Advertising Substantiation (accessed October 7, 2026). The next

84
00:04:28.372 --> 00:04:31.605
useful question is not “What can this tool do?” It

85
00:04:31.605 --> 00:04:34.838
is “What do I know, what do I not know,

86
00:04:34.838 --> 00:04:37.425
and what is the smallest responsible next step?”
